  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 952 PM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nPREDOMINATELY DRY THIS WEEKEND:\n\nHigh pressure builds across Colorado this weekend. This will \nlead to a drier weekend, with daytime highs warming by a few \ndegrees. Moisture begins to overtake the region on Sunday, but \nthis will mostly lead to increasing cloud cover. \n\nWET WEEK AHEAD:\n\nBy Monday, the forecast becomes a bit more challenging. \nSouthwest follow over the Great Basin will begin to draw in \nmoisture from a pair of tropical systems, including Polo. PWATs \nwill return to upwards of 200% of normal, particularly in \nsouthwest Colorado and southeast Utah. This indicates the start \nof another wet period through at least midweek. It is important \nto note that some discrepancies exist between models, with the \nGFS consistently showing a drier solution, whereas the Euro \ncontinues to be more bullish with the moisture. Cluster \nsolutions also continue to be inconsistent on how to handle the \nmoisture in addition to the upstream trough, so confidence \nremains low on a final solution. Bottom line is that the start \nof next week looks to be wetter, but whether this will be a \nsignificant/anomalous event for the Four Corners area is yet to \nbe determined. Stay tuned for how this forecast begins to \ndevelop. Please see the Hydrology section below for more on the \npotential for flooding with this system. \n\nIn addition to this moisture, daytime highs will drop by a few \ndegrees come Monday, which also means overnight lows will do the\nsame. If you're catching what we're putting down, that would \nindicate snowflakes returning to the forecast for some our \nhighest peaks and passes.",
